WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:05.890
Do you remember when we were kids, our parents
or maybe teachers would tell us, hey, be careful

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:10.520
with peer pressure, because other peers could
pressure you to do things that you don't want

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:12.400
to do, so it's a bad thing, right?

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:18.350
Now as you age, and you become an adult, what
if I told you peer pressure is a wonderful

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:21.860
thing, as long as you choose the right peers.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:23.490
It's the most incredible thing.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:26.620
Because they could pressure you to do great
things with your life that you wouldn't have

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:29.030
done if you didn't have the right peer pressure.

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:34.150
So, based on that, if you think about you
do studies, you will realize that your friends,

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:37.930
your close friends could dictate your diet,
your health, your marriage, your relationships,

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:41.809
your finances, your happiness,, all of that
they can dictate, depending on the type of

00:00:41.809 --> 00:00:42.809
friends you choose.

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:45.289
This is why today's episode is called, "How
to Choose Great Friends."

00:00:45.289 --> 00:00:49.739
So now, I'm going to dive in to this topic
a little bit deeper and kind of process and

00:00:49.739 --> 00:00:55.520
ask the question, hey, why is it that some
of our friends in 6th grade, we're no longer

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:56.520
friends with?

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:00.239
Why is it that my friend in high school that
I played Nintendo with, and I was constantly

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:05.030
playing Zelda or whatever the game you played,
Final Fantasy something or Fester's Quest

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:08.810
for some of you guys that played, Super Mario
Brothers - how come I'm not friends with them

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:09.810
anymore?

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:11.149
How come I don't spend all the time with them
anymore?

00:01:11.149 --> 00:01:14.170
I saw them every single day for three years.

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:15.640
I don't do it anymore.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:16.700
Is that a bad thing?

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:17.700
We're going to process that.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:18.700
So today, solve for x.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:19.700
What is x?

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:20.729
How to choose great friends.

00:01:20.729 --> 00:01:22.370
Let's process it.

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:24.619
Friendship goes through three different phases.

00:01:24.619 --> 00:01:27.040
Phase 1, 12-18 years old.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:28.360
This is when we're trying to be accepted.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:30.479
You're in school, you just kind of want to
be cool.

00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:31.929
You're just happy you have friends.

00:01:31.929 --> 00:01:35.310
Matter of fact, your parents are somewhat
happy that you have some friends, because

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:36.310
you're not a loner, right?

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:38.280
You're like, "Hey, I have five friends.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.109
These guys are willing to be friends with
me.

00:01:40.109 --> 00:01:41.109
This is so cool.

00:01:41.109 --> 00:01:43.100
I'm being accepted."

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:48.009
At this phase, 19-29, phase two, this is experimenting
phase.

00:01:48.009 --> 00:01:49.399
You're experimenting with people.

00:01:49.399 --> 00:01:51.049
You're experimenting with who you get along
with.

00:01:51.049 --> 00:01:53.710
You're experimenting so you kind of learning
about yourself.

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:57.619
And then phase three, is 30+, this is clarity.

00:01:57.619 --> 00:02:01.549
You kind of have an idea what types of people
you want to be friends with.

00:02:01.549 --> 00:02:03.310
What you like, what you don't like.

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:08.520
You kind of have molded into somebody that
hopefully at that age you're somewhat self-aware

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:09.520
of yourself.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.370
Now, a few things that we go through here
[phase 1]: Sports, friends, our friends, sports,

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:15.550
and video games.

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:21.050
Phase two, partying, college, military, you
meet them in college, you hang out in college,

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:23.580
you go to clubs in college, you drink together,
you party together.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:25.720
In the military you party together.

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:26.970
Maybe afterwards you get a job.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:30.380
You've got a company you're working at with
a job and one guy becomes your friend because

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:32.260
you guys got started at the same time.

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:34.400
So those relationships are being started here.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.920
And then in phase three, you're getting married
possibly.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:38.160
Maybe you have kids.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.920
You now have a job, you realize how competitive
it is.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.050
Maybe you've moved to a complete different
place so you're no longer connected to your

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:46.780
friends that you were because you're living
in a different place, different state because

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:48.470
of a promotion you took.

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:49.710
So these [phases] change, right?

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:50.930
Now here becomes a question.

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:52.580
Here becomes a question.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:57.820
Some people still are friends with their high
school friends, when they were 16 years old,

00:02:57.820 --> 00:02:59.280
and they're still friends with them today.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:00.990
Why is that?

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:04.850
And why is that that some people who were
friends there, those people who were inseparable,

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:09.060
they can't even get along or sit down together
for a 20-minute conversation.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.510
Have you had a conversation with one of your
old friends and you're sitting down with them

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:16.620
and you're like, for 15 minutes we talk and
we have nothing in common.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:17.620
What happened?

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:20.120
At 17 it was all day every day.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:21.650
We've got nothing in common.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:22.650
Why is that?

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:23.650
Here's why.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:25.340
It's the Evolution of Friendship.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:27.900
The evolution of friendship is very interesting.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:34.620
If you, at 16 years old are the same with
interests, goals, whatever it is, you're the

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:39.390
same at 32 years old - same, same, you get
along.

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:43.730
If John is the same as you are, at 32 as he
was at 16, you get along.

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:52.170
If John, at 16, or 21, by 32, he decided to
go become an entrepreneur businessman, or

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:57.720
a lawyer, or an executive or a career path,
and now his friend is also on the same page,

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:03.020
he's changed, he's not the 21-year-old, he's
a different 31-year-old, he's changed, they

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:04.020
get along.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:10.950
But, if John at 21 was the best at partying,
and so was Bobby, but at 31 John is now running

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:14.770
a business, he's an attorney, he's an executive,
he's doing very well for himself, but his

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:18.230
friend is still the same, same and change
don't get along.

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:20.190
There's nothing else in common.

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:22.520
So this is when you see a friend from high
school and you're like wait a minute, how

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:25.160
come they're not hanging out together anymore?

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:27.040
Why has this evolution of friends taken?

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:28.320
Am I a bad person?

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:29.610
Are they a bad person?

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:30.610
No.

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:31.610
Neither is good or bad.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:33.660
They're just no longer a connection.

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:35.170
There's got to be a connection.

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:40.590
So this is very important to identify, because
in life, as you get to this phase [3] and

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:44.120
some of you may be watching and you're maybe
at this phase [1], and you're kind of like,

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:45.120
man, I'm just trying to be accepted.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:46.870
I want a girlfriend to hold my hand.

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:47.870
Right?

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:48.870
And you're going to go through some of this.

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:50.710
Some of you may be at this age [2], right?

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:54.690
I remember being 26 years old, an Asian mentor
told me something fascinating.

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:59.760
He said, "Patrick, at 27, 28, as a man, weird
things are going to happen.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:01.660
Don't take it too seriously.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:05.580
You're going to start really asking some weird
questions and have too much pressure on yourself.

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:06.580
Don't take it too seriously.

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:08.730
And that's exactly what happened to me.

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:12.350
Because 28, 29, sometimes even men and women
they go through the phase if they're single

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:14.300
and not married yet, is it too late, baby?

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:15.340
I should have gotten married.

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:16.580
My friends got married.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:17.580
They already have two kids.

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:18.580
I don't have kids.

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:20.270
It's all compare, compare, compare, compare.

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:22.780
You're trying to live everybody else's life.

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:24.560
Maybe your first marriage didn't work out.

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:26.020
Compare, compare, compare, right?

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:31.210
So now if you're here [3], and you're trying
to get clear about life, this thing should

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:32.210
be very clear.

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:34.500
Let me help you out by what I mean by this
part here.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:39.340
There's three different types of people that
benefit us, for us to hang out with.

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:40.680
People that are above us in our life.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:43.180
These are people that we aspire to be one
day.

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:45.020
People at the same level as us.

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:47.010
And people below us.

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:49.790
When I say below, I don't mean demeaning,
inferior, just below us.

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:52.600
They want to have our life, per se.

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:54.600
What are the benefits for each category?

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:57.680
People you hang out with that are above you,
what is the benefit?

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:00.800
You aspire to be like them, so they challenge
you, they push you, you go to a restaurant

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:04.420
or party, you're around people that make - I
remember the first time I went to a party,

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:06.460
this man invited me to his house party.

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:07.460
He was turning 50.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:08.970
I went to his house in Westlake.

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:13.240
I'm 22, 23 years old, and I went over there.

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:19.070
Everyone pulled up in Ferraris, Lamborghinis,
Jaguars, I'm pulling up in my Ford Focus,

00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:20.070
when I pulled up.

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:22.650
And I parked all the way in the back and I
go inside.

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:28.240
This one guy's showing me his 2002, 2003 Harley
that he had bought, just a fancy Harley that

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:29.240
he had silver.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:30.240
I'll never forget it.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:34.100
Drop-dead gorgeous model, and everyone I'm
talking to is a multi-millionaire.

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:39.780
Everyone is celebrity, Hollywood actor, actress,
business executive, and I'm not the guy.

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:40.940
But this person invites me.

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:42.100
So I go in.

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:45.710
It was the most awkward feeling because I
got - people would ask me, what do you do?

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:48.520
I would say, "I'm a salesperson at Bally's
Total Fitness."

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:49.780
Who is this guy?

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:51.310
I work at Bally.

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:52.510
Which Bally's, the casino?

00:06:52.510 --> 00:06:54.780
No, the Bally's at a gym.

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:56.680
People thought I was working at a casino.

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:00.760
So I hit a point where it was so uncomfortable
for me I got on a call, I called my friends

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:06.970
to go shoot pool at the pool place in San
Fernando, called Charles Billiards, I don't

00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:07.970
know which one.

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:10.150
Charles Billiards was in Glendale.

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:16.010
Billiards in San Fernando was I think . . . anyways,
so I went to this place, because I'm like,

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:17.020
"Guy's, we've got to go shoot pool.

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:20.670
I was just at a party with a bunch of millionaires
who want to brag about the kind of money they

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:21.670
have."

00:07:21.670 --> 00:07:25.470
I'm driving on the 101, while I'm driving
on the 101, I'll never forget this, I'm passing

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:29.690
the 101 on Westlake, there's a Mercedes Benz
dealership, I'm passing by.

00:07:29.690 --> 00:07:32.320
Pat, what the hell are you thinking?

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:33.610
This is your opportunity.

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:35.550
Turn around!

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:37.290
I turned around, exited.

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:38.840
I go back to Westlake.

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:40.410
My exit was Canaan.

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:42.260
Canaan, I go up.

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:44.020
And I go to this guy's house.

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:46.780
I was the last person to leave the house.

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:48.060
I helped wash dishes.

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:49.140
I helped whatever.

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:50.740
Hey, what do you need help with?

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:51.740
Anything I can help with?

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:55.172
I just wanted to hear what these guys were
thinking and talking about.

00:07:55.172 --> 00:07:56.172
And I was fascinated.

00:07:56.172 --> 00:07:57.820
I became great friends with them.

00:07:57.820 --> 00:07:59.930
The guy, some went to prison for nine years.

00:07:59.930 --> 00:08:07.800
I went and visited him in prison and Men's
Colony, in San Luis Obispo, right by Morrow

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:15.639
Bay, I think it's called Morrow Bay, it's
not Palos Verdes, it's right by Santa Barbara.

00:08:15.639 --> 00:08:16.950
Real nice place.

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:19.620
Anyways, he was above me at the time.

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:21.530
This is a man that was making 600K.

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:23.250
I was aspiring to be like him.

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:24.250
He challenged me.

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:25.470
The people pushed me.

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:26.720
They changed me.

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:28.500
I got a taste of it that day.

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:33.719
A former military veteran making 800 bucks
a month was around people making 800 bucks

00:08:33.719 --> 00:08:34.719
every two hours.

00:08:34.719 --> 00:08:36.870
That's a whole different story.

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:38.199
It's mindset being shifted.

00:08:38.199 --> 00:08:39.779
Oh my gosh.

00:08:39.779 --> 00:08:42.509
The benefits of hanging around people that
are the same level as you, you realize you're

00:08:42.509 --> 00:08:43.509
not alone.

00:08:43.509 --> 00:08:46.499
It's very important to be around other people
that are like you as well because you realize

00:08:46.499 --> 00:08:49.600
if you're married, going through a struggle,
it's great to be hanging out with other people

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:51.639
that are married going through struggles because
you're not alone.

00:08:51.639 --> 00:08:54.410
If you're an executive moving up, it's good
to hang out with others.

00:08:54.410 --> 00:08:56.999
If you're running a business, it's good to
hang out with other people running a startup

00:08:56.999 --> 00:08:58.750
business because you're going through the
same issues.

00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:00.360
You are not alone.

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:04.029
You're trying to hire people, fire people,
funding, running out of money, going out of

00:09:04.029 --> 00:09:10.210
business, fights with wife, it's very - this
is why organizations like YPO, Vistage, these

00:09:10.210 --> 00:09:13.889
guys do very well, Inc. conventions, they
do very well because it's a place for you

00:09:13.889 --> 00:09:16.569
to go and be around other entrepreneurs that
are struggling with the same things.

00:09:16.569 --> 00:09:20.120
And people below you, when you hang out with
them, it makes you realize that you have value

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:21.840
to bring to other people, so you can lift
people up.

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:23.899
That feels good when you do that.

00:09:23.899 --> 00:09:27.439
Now the bad thing if you do too much with
people below you, they pull you down.

00:09:27.439 --> 00:09:32.079
If you only hang around people that make you
feel superior, your ego goes up, but you actually

00:09:32.079 --> 00:09:35.009
decrease in your status in life, and you go
lower and lower and lower.

00:09:35.009 --> 00:09:38.189
And if you only hang around people that are
the same level as you, guess what happens?

00:09:38.189 --> 00:09:39.500
No peer pressure.

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:40.880
What peer pressure?

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:44.759
If I have people around me that are the same
status as me, what peer pressure do I have?

00:09:44.759 --> 00:09:46.329
They don't have any aspirations.

00:09:46.329 --> 00:09:49.050
Peer pressure is boom, up there, above you.

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:50.970
That's a lot of peer pressure.

00:09:50.970 --> 00:09:52.329
A lot of peer pressure.

00:09:52.329 --> 00:09:56.959
And most people don't like it, that's why
by far, the person that people talk about

00:09:56.959 --> 00:09:59.209
the most is always here [above].

00:09:59.209 --> 00:10:00.709
This is the people we make rumors about.

00:10:00.709 --> 00:10:02.600
People want to talk about these guys here.

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:03.600
All the time.

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:06.990
All he cares about, all she cares about, all
they care about.

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:12.189
So now, now that we have figure out this whole
thing here, phases we go through, evolution,

00:10:12.189 --> 00:10:17.370
same, same, change, change, same change, above
you, same, below you, now you kind of sit

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:20.149
there and you say, Okay, so knowing that,
now what do I do?

00:10:20.149 --> 00:10:22.700
How do I pick and choose my friends?

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:27.730
What do I do now for myself, because some
of the stats you'll look at, a study was done

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:29.220
in Britain.

00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:34.860
This study said that the average person ends
up having 396 friends in their life that they

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:35.860
become friends with.

00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:40.640
I'm not talking Facebook friends, I've got
5,000 people I reached a max!

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:41.829
I have 5,000 friends.

00:10:41.829 --> 00:10:42.850
Those are not friends.

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:45.819
Those are just, you know, it's not even acquaintances.

00:10:45.819 --> 00:10:48.679
You know, 10% are maybe acquaintances.

00:10:48.679 --> 00:10:52.119
Some of them are just following because hey,
we met somewhere and we're hanging out, so

00:10:52.119 --> 00:10:53.119
it's an acquaintance.

00:10:53.119 --> 00:10:56.490
But 396, we become friends with.

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:59.509
Out of that, one in 12 stays with you.

00:10:59.509 --> 00:11:03.730
So about 33 friends stay with us.

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:09.100
Out of those 33 friends, Euripides once said,
"One loyal friend is worth more than 10,000

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:10.100
relatives."

00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:12.170
One loyal friend is worth more than 10,000
relatives.

00:11:12.170 --> 00:11:16.180
You've got a lot of family members that are
your blood, they act like they're your enemies

00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:17.459
sometimes, right?

00:11:17.459 --> 00:11:19.769
You know, they don't want to see you win,
they spread rumors, and you have this one

00:11:19.769 --> 00:11:21.670
friend that's just loyal.

00:11:21.670 --> 00:11:22.670
He accepts you.

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:23.670
She accepts you.

00:11:23.670 --> 00:11:26.840
You're like, man, you make me feel good, but
you also push me and challenge me and you

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:30.290
know the whole picture you see, a true friend
doesn't stab you in the back; they stab you

00:11:30.290 --> 00:11:31.290
in the front.

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:32.290
They tell you in your face.

00:11:32.290 --> 00:11:33.820
You shouldn't have done what you just did.

00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:34.820
That was not cool.

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:35.820
You don't talk to a girl like that.

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:38.379
You don't talk to your husband like that.

00:11:38.379 --> 00:11:41.619
They talk to you directly to your face as
well, and you like that.

00:11:41.619 --> 00:11:43.480
That's the one loyal friend you find as valuable.

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:45.439
So how do you find these types of friends
out there?

00:11:45.439 --> 00:11:48.769
So a couple things to keep in mind.

00:11:48.769 --> 00:11:50.589
#1, raise your standards.

00:11:50.589 --> 00:11:52.010
Very simple.

00:11:52.010 --> 00:11:54.819
Raise your standards, on who becomes a great
friend to you.

00:11:54.819 --> 00:11:59.490
When I say raise your standards, you choose
who becomes a great friend that you want to

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:00.490
be friends with.

00:12:00.490 --> 00:12:04.699
The standards comes over here, you choose
who it is.

00:12:04.699 --> 00:12:08.139
And how do you raise your standards, by being
selective on who you want your friends to

00:12:08.139 --> 00:12:09.139
be.

00:12:09.139 --> 00:12:10.300
So how do you become selective?

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:14.290
You know, as you become friends to other people,
and you're kind of going through, look at

00:12:14.290 --> 00:12:16.920
it as a filtering process.

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:21.179
You've got to be out there around other people,
because studies have shown, people with larger

00:12:21.179 --> 00:12:22.829
networks live longer.

00:12:22.829 --> 00:12:25.980
Because people with larger networks have more
people that support them.

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:27.189
Hey, we believe in you.

00:12:27.189 --> 00:12:28.189
We're praying for you.

00:12:28.189 --> 00:12:29.300
Everything's going to be okay.

00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:30.300
It's fine.

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:35.779
And you get 75 text messages when you're sick,
is better than when you get one text message.

00:12:35.779 --> 00:12:37.899
When it's your birthday, they say, "I love
you man.

00:12:37.899 --> 00:12:38.899
Happy Birthday.

00:12:38.899 --> 00:12:39.899
You always made me feel so good."

00:12:39.899 --> 00:12:41.170
You feel good, you want to live longer.

00:12:41.170 --> 00:12:45.730
This is why grandfather who is 74-years-old
with five grandkids looks at it and says,

00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:48.509
"I want to live 10 more years, even though
I've had 13 heart attacks" - a.k.a.

00:12:48.509 --> 00:12:49.509
my father.

00:12:49.509 --> 00:12:55.779
He's had 13 heart attacks, six angiograms,
three angioplasties, three stents in his heart.

00:12:55.779 --> 00:12:56.779
He's still living.

00:12:56.779 --> 00:13:00.050
He had his heart attack in 1993, 23 years
ago.

00:13:00.050 --> 00:13:04.489
But there's a reason to live, when you have
a larger network of friends, you want to live

00:13:04.489 --> 00:13:05.489
longer.

00:13:05.489 --> 00:13:10.619
So very simple, be selective, raise your standards,
find friends that have common goals.

00:13:10.619 --> 00:13:13.449
If you have aspirations, they also have aspirations.

00:13:13.449 --> 00:13:14.680
Find friends that have common interests.

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:15.680
If you like sports, they like sports.

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:17.529
If you like politics, they like politics.

00:13:17.529 --> 00:13:18.560
I have friends that are Democrats.

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:19.800
I have friends that are Republicans.

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:20.989
I have friends that are Independent.

00:13:20.989 --> 00:13:22.540
I have friends that don't believe in God.

00:13:22.540 --> 00:13:23.569
I have friends that believe in God.

00:13:23.569 --> 00:13:24.569
I have friends that are Christian.

00:13:24.569 --> 00:13:25.569
I have friends that are Jewish.

00:13:25.569 --> 00:13:28.429
I have friends that are Atheist, Agnostic.

00:13:28.429 --> 00:13:35.360
That part doesn't necessarily dictate my friendship
with them, as much as if I believe in living

00:13:35.360 --> 00:13:40.230
a life with character, integrity, and they
have that as well, that's values.

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:45.709
Values doesn't necessarily mean exact religion,
exact political party, that doesn't mean anything.

00:13:45.709 --> 00:13:48.610
It means common values and common interests.

00:13:48.610 --> 00:13:53.449
And then on top of that, the other thing I
would add to that is, somebody that celebrates

00:13:53.449 --> 00:13:58.720
your victories with you, somebody that celebrates
their victories with you, meaning when you're

00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:01.069
winning, they don't always go back and talk
about their own victories.

00:14:01.069 --> 00:14:03.239
Hey, you won't believe what happened to my
kid today.

00:14:03.239 --> 00:14:05.060
You know, let me tell you what, oh my gosh
that's so cool.

00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:07.329
Instantly, "Well let me tell you what my kid
did!"

00:14:07.329 --> 00:14:08.329
C'mon.

00:14:08.329 --> 00:14:10.149
I mean, it's like I'm telling you about my
kid, man.

00:14:10.149 --> 00:14:11.149
Just celebrate with me.

00:14:11.149 --> 00:14:12.540
I'm just trying to be transparent.

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:14.459
I want somebody to say, "Oh my gosh!"

00:14:14.459 --> 00:14:16.230
Be interested, you know.

00:14:16.230 --> 00:14:20.459
And you know some people can't take that,
and some will just immediately - that's maybe

00:14:20.459 --> 00:14:24.500
a friend, but it's not like a friend, friend
you're choosing that celebrates their victories

00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:25.500
with you.

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:30.839
Acceptance, be a great friend, is what I would
tell you as well when you are a great friend

00:14:30.839 --> 00:14:35.220
yourself to other people, they will be the
same to you as well.

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:39.430
In life, look, quantity, quality, this is
the one area where quality matters.

00:14:39.430 --> 00:14:41.309
It's great to have a large network of friends.

00:14:41.309 --> 00:14:42.309
Don't get me wrong.

00:14:42.309 --> 00:14:48.160
It's very cool to have a large network of
friends where you know, I had my father-in-law,

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:54.519
my sister's husband's father, when he passed
away, I will never forget when I went to his

00:14:54.519 --> 00:14:58.579
funeral, and they had to do the funeral three,
four, five different times, because of how

00:14:58.579 --> 00:15:00.819
many people wanted to come and visit his funeral.

00:15:00.819 --> 00:15:06.449
He was a Walter Cronkite of the Persian community.

00:15:06.449 --> 00:15:09.429
Mr. Sabetimani.

00:15:09.429 --> 00:15:11.689
Every time I was around him, he was so kind.

00:15:11.689 --> 00:15:14.149
Everyone loved this man.

00:15:14.149 --> 00:15:15.149
He was so kind.

00:15:15.149 --> 00:15:17.149
He was loved by the world.

00:15:17.149 --> 00:15:20.240
He was one of Shah's favorite people, he spent
so much time with the shah.

00:15:20.240 --> 00:15:24.779
He's not around with us, God bless his soul,
my brother in-law, I call him a saint.

00:15:24.779 --> 00:15:28.839
He's the only guy I met when my sister was
dating, I met this guy and said, this is the

00:15:28.839 --> 00:15:29.839
guy!

00:15:29.839 --> 00:15:30.839
He's a saint.

00:15:30.839 --> 00:15:31.839
I've never met a man like this before.

00:15:31.839 --> 00:15:33.670
And when his father passed away, it was very
difficult.

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:37.290
When we went to his funeral, thousands of
people showed up.

00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:43.579
And my brother-in-law took it like, I mean,
Siamak led the entire thing; I was so proud

00:15:43.579 --> 00:15:44.579
of him, when I saw him.

00:15:44.579 --> 00:15:45.769
I was watching his entire mannerisms.

00:15:45.769 --> 00:15:50.069
Like, he said, I am running this family, and
I'm going to make sure I do my part to represent

00:15:50.069 --> 00:15:51.699
my father properly.

00:15:51.699 --> 00:15:57.449
But here's a man that died, with a large network
of people that cared for him, and he had a

00:15:57.449 --> 00:16:01.019
group of friends that also cared for him as
well that were very close to him.

00:16:01.019 --> 00:16:03.439
That's really a big part of life.

00:16:03.439 --> 00:16:06.339
If you're going to make millions, you're going
to make billions, and you have nobody to talk

00:16:06.339 --> 00:16:10.249
to about, nobody to share your struggles and
victories with, it's lonely.

00:16:10.249 --> 00:16:11.759
What's the purpose of it?

00:16:11.759 --> 00:16:12.980
You've got to be able to do those things.

00:16:12.980 --> 00:16:17.869
So as important as it is, to marry the right
person, as important as it is to marry the

00:16:17.869 --> 00:16:24.339
right person, if you choose to get married,
it is just as important to befriend the right

00:16:24.339 --> 00:16:29.420
people that give you the right kind of peer
pressure that challenge you to improve, and

00:16:29.420 --> 00:16:34.139
grow, and just make sure when you find friends
like that, let me tell you, make sure when

00:16:34.139 --> 00:16:38.850
you find friends like that, make sure you
keep them, for a very, very long time.

00:16:38.850 --> 00:16:44.239
Hopefully, till the day you die, because friends
like that are very, very, very difficult.

00:16:44.239 --> 00:16:49.449
Listen, very, very, very difficult to find,
when you do find them.

00:16:49.449 --> 00:16:52.880
So this is my processing on friendship.

00:16:52.880 --> 00:16:55.869
You know, conversations like this, people
ask, Mario and I will sit down and say, "Let's

00:16:55.869 --> 00:16:57.290
process this whole friendship thing.

